  algorithm: {
    said: [
      { d: '2023-07-12', src: 'Investor deck Jul 2023, slide 14', claim: 'IPO algorithm = three drivers: grow share in existing categories, enter adjacent and new categories, international expansion. No TAM number.', q: 'GROW SHARE IN EXISTING CATEGORIES ... ENTER ADJACENT AND NEW CATEGORIES ... INTERNATIONAL EXPANSION', url: sq(196264, 86191, 14) },
      { d: '2023-08-24', src: 'Q2 2023 call', claim: 'Three pillars, with new sub-categories framed as "more use occasions and more products per household". Management declines a long-term top-line algorithm.', q: 'Two, expanding across new subcategories and adjacencies, which drives more use occasions and more products per household for the brands.', url: tq(2891108, 91000, '421.15') },
      { d: '2024-02-15', src: 'Q4 2023 call', claim: 'Interim CFO: the 2024 guide of 7–9% is "probably not a bad template", nothing official.', q: 'the 7-9% top line and, you know, probably not a, not a bad template, you know, kind of, as we look forward.', url: tq(2798539, 129667, '3483.88') },
      { d: '2024-03-28', src: 'Investor deck Mar 2024, slide 17', claim: '"TAM Expansion" becomes the deck label for the new-categories pillar.', q: 'More Retailers + More SKUs + TAM Expansion', url: sq(2530183, 534594, 17) },
      { d: '2024-09-04', src: 'Goldman Sachs conf. Sep 2024', claim: 'Three buckets: the big core base, emerging categories entered 2–3 years ago (beauty, outdoor cooking), brand-new categories.', q: 'The second bucket is these categories that are emerging, categories for us that we entered into kind of two, three years ago.', url: tq(2641089, 225981, '206.4') },
      { d: '2024-12-04', src: 'Morgan Stanley conf. Dec 2024', claim: 'The market grew ~2.5% a year over 16 years while SN compounded at 20%.', q: 'And I think that we\'ve been in a market that over the last 16 years has grown at about 2.5% a year. We\'ve grown at 20% during that period of time.', url: tq(2579265, 258842, '67.32') },
      { d: '2025-02-13', src: 'Q4 2024 call', claim: 'First explicit long-term positioning: a double-digit grower; pillars may "toggle" by year.', q: 'You know, we\'ve said that we believe that we are a long-term double-digit growth company.', url: tq(3765661, 238603, '2415.48') },
      { d: '2025-03-11', src: 'BofA conf. Mar 2025', claim: 'Algorithm by geography: domestic 6–7% + international 15–16% = double digits. In 2024 the largest portion of +$1.3B came from existing categories.', q: 'can the domestic business grow 6-7%, and can the international business grow 15-16%? Together, you have got a double-digit growth business.', url: tq(2528581, 324650, '838.86') },
      { d: '2026-02-11', src: 'Q4 2025 call', claim: 'Category expansion tied explicitly to growing the addressable market.', q: 'Category expansion is one of our most foundational differentiators. It grows our addressable market and reshapes how consumers perceive the Shark Ninja brands.', url: tq(2803871, 399981, '731.02') },
      { d: '2026-08-05', src: 'Q2 2026 call', claim: 'CEO calls the "viral new subcategories drive growth" view a <b>misperception</b>.', q: 'There is a misperception that these subcategories are an outsized part of the business that requires enormous growth to drive total sales', url: tq(3968466, 661459, '253.1') },
      { d: '2026-08-05', src: 'Q2 2026 call', claim: 'First numeric algorithm: existing categories MSD–HSD (3-yr average), plus international, plus new categories = double digits. New deck slides added.', q: 'On average, over the last three years, our existing categories typically grow mid-to-high single digits. Layer on international expansion, layer on new category launches, add those three together. You can get a double-digit growth profile.', url: tq(3968466, 661459, '318.46') },
      { d: '2026-08-11', src: 'Canaccord conf. Aug 2026', claim: 'IR: new materials built to counter the narrative; ~20% of three-year growth from products two years old or less. CFO: new categories are "not the story".', q: 'it turns out only about 20% of our growth contribution over the last three years comes from new products that have been released two years or less.', url: tq(4003829, 718762, '473.4') },
      { d: '2026-09-15', src: 'Goldman Sachs conf. Sep 2026', claim: 'Core MSD–HSD + new products + international = "mid to high teens"; industry compounding at 1.5% a year since 2008.', q: 'underlying all of this is a really healthy core base business growing at mid- to high- single digits. Then you layer on top of that the new products, and you layer on top of that the international growth, and it translates into a really strong growth algorithm, in mid to high teens.', url: tq(4171240, 743347, '42.26') }
    ],
    deck: {
      d: '2026-08-05', src: 'Investor deck Aug 2026, slide 20', url: sq(3966451, 661459, 20),
      existing: 40, international: 40, newCats: 20, launchYear: 10,
      launchSrc: 'Investor deck Aug 2026, slide 21', launchUrl: sq(3966451, 661459, 21),
      note: '<b>Slide 20</b>, "All three pillars of SharkNinja\'s growth strategy contribute meaningfully to net sales growth": share of net sales growth, <i>3-year average 2023–2025, approximate</i> — <b>~40% Existing Categories</b> (launched 2+ years earlier, Domestic segment), <b>~20% New &amp; Adjacent</b> (launched in the last 2 years, any geography), <b>~40% International</b> (categories 2+ years old, International segment). ' +
        '<b>Slide 21</b>: in the launch year, new categories contribute <b>~10%</b> of net sales growth vs ~90% from existing categories — "SharkNinja\'s net sales growth is powered by the size and strength of our core. Our large, diversified existing businesses drive the topline, and new categories eventually mature into that core, fortifying it further".'
    },
    read: '<b>Where it confirms the thesis.</b> Entering categories is an explicit, standing commitment (two a year, delivered every year) and management ties it directly to the TAM: "It grows our addressable market". Categories mature into core franchises (CREAMi from a $50M US category to 30+ countries), so a launch keeps contributing after it stops counting as "new". The industry grew ~1.5–2.5% a year for 16+ years while SN compounded ~20%: the growth is share and category creation, not market lift. ' +
      '<b>Where it complicates it.</b> Management\'s own decomposition gives new &amp; adjacent categories only <b>≈20%</b> of 2023–25 growth and <b>≈10%</b> in the launch year; ≈40% is existing categories and ≈40% international. The Q2 2026 slides were built to push back on exactly the "new categories drive growth" reading, and the CFO says dependency on new categories "is not the story". One nuance in favour of breadth: much of the ≈40% international is existing categories rolled into new countries (France and Germany went from roughly 10 categories to "up over 50%" in a year), so category count matters twice. Yet on Sep 15 2026 the CEO still led with "$10 billion of TAM" added.'
  },

